314286,"type":"genre","name":"Synth Funk","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":211,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":50337978407967,"type":"genre","name":"Smooth Soul","n":5},{"id":112672968317071,"type":"genre","name":"Boogie","n":3},{"id":269705969260495,"type":"genre","name":"Disco","n":2},{"id":30122583811636,"type":"genre","name":"Soul","n":2},{"id":176578125596945,"type":"genre","name":"Contemporary R&B","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Rising R&B Star (1981)","body":"In 1981, Luther Vandross was recognized as the fastest rising star in R&B, following a long climb through the music industry. \"In 1981, Luther Vandros was the fastest rising star in R&B, but he'd had a long slow climb.\"","year":1981,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4eec154465cd600c4d67"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Background Music Career (1970s)","body":"Before his breakthrough as a solo artist, Vandross spent years \"toiling in the background\" of the music business during the 1970s, working as a backing vocalist, singer of commercial jingles, and a vocal arranger. \"Back in the 70s, Vandros was a backing vocalist, singer of commercial jingles, and a vocal arranger.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4eec154465cd600c4d67"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Dream to Work with Aretha Franklin (Early 1980s)","body":"In an interview with Rolling Stone magazine after the success of \"Never Too Much,\" Vandross revealed his dream to collaborate with his hero, Aretha Franklin. This dream was realized when Clive Davis read the interview and arranged their studio sessions. \"Vandros gave an interview to Rolling Stone magazine in which he revealed his dream to work with his hero, Aritha Franklin.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4eec154465cd600c4d67"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Early Jingle Career","body":"Before achieving his big break as a renowned recording artist, Luther Vandross sustained his career and paid his bills by singing numerous jingles. His vocal talents were featured in advertisements for a diverse range of brands, including Coca-Cola, Kentucky Fried Chicken, and Juicy Fruit gum. \"Luther Vandros, whom we discussed just last month in our Aretha Franklin episode, paid his bills before his big break by singing numerous jingles for brands ranging from Coca-Cola to Kentucky Fried Chicken to juicy fruit gum.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"I’d Like to Teach the World to Buy Edition Part 1","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"I’d Like to Teach the World to Buy Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f1264fe6d21276e0cc5"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Signed by Tony DeFries","body":"Luther Vandross was later signed on by manager Tony DeFries, whose management company, Main Man, had previously managed several artists orbiting David Bowie.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Mighty Managers of Rock","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PzyvXmDKFUc"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Studio Collaboration with Aretha Franklin (Early 1980s)","body":"Vandross and Franklin had a \"combustible\" dynamic in the studio due to their strong opinions on singing. He encouraged her to experiment with new techniques, including scat-like singing, while Franklin assertively maintained her legendary status. \"Luther and Aritha proved combustible in the studio, both headstrong performers with strong opinions about singing.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Say a Little Prayer Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4eec154465cd600c4d67"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best New Artist (1982)","body":"Nominated.","year":1982,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1453643"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"star on Hollywood Walk of Fame","body":"Won.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q17985761"}]}],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":165751501372738,"type":"album","name":"Never Too Much","work_year":1981,"list_id":215424088090262,"list":"AOTY Must Hear 1980s","src":"charts","rank":165,"year":1981,"note":null,"yt":"o6vCn2sBbBE","scaruffi":null},{"id":165751501372738,"type":"album","name":"Never Too Much","list_id":168313144576350,"list":"RYM Top — Dance","src":"musthear","rank":132,"year":1981,"note":null,"yt":"o6vCn2sBbBE","scaruffi":null},{"id":165751501372738,"type":"album","name":"Never Too Much","list_id":94986022996747,"list":"RYM Top — Dance — Boogie","src":"musthear","rank":6,"year":1981,"note":null,"yt":"o6vCn2sBbBE","scaruffi":null},{"id":165751501372738,"type":"album","name":"Never Too Much","list_id":200368112062640,"list":"RYM Top — Dance — Disco","src":"musthear","rank":20,"year":1981,"